:step/math-academy-id 32916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176831 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177049 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a point $(\\rho,\\theta,\\phi)$ in spherical polar coordinates,\n\n- $\\rho \\geq 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- ${\\color{black}\\theta}\\in [0, 2\\pi)$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\\phi \\in [0, \\pi]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nWhen we fix one of these coordinates, we obtain some simple surfaces. Let's describe them:\n\n- First, let's consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\rho = c,\n$$\n\nfor $c \\gt 0.$ This equation describes the set of points on a surface whose distance from the origin equals $c.$ So, we obtain a sphere of radius $c$ centered at $O.$ \n\n![](images/1a/1aaecd51b8699f9a537496fa3bd581e153360eec3ef0e4bff4748c9ba3254a55.png)\n\n- Next, consider the equation \n\n$$\n\\theta=c,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $c\\in [0,2\\pi).$ This equation describes the set of points that make an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we obtain a *half-plane* whose boundary is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/5a/5a4e9d6481978b148ae696b73e75859808095856aacb269a2069dd3265be8684.png)\n\n- Finally, consider the equation\n\n$$\n\\phi = c,\n$$\n\nwhere $c\\in (0,\\pi).$ This equation describes the set of points that make an angle of $c$ radians with the positive $z$-axis. Therefore, we obtain a *half-cone* whose axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/cc/ccae1dd448e3e47ac9488f46302122c140c5efef3bd598b3eabd2ec00ded0f08.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "cb9503c8-e1b8-5345-8876-3dc47eb3cc7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5990 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "382a508a-8216-55b4-ab13-c25fd47ad39b" :step/math-academy-id 36564 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176700}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176066 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175711 :question/id #uuid "e4f2ef63-cc21-5aeb-b1c4-6a7a4d40e81a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12411"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fde60ded-ec05-5a2c-8844-26e8e02c9afb" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176396} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281347} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401295}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Part of a Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "1123a203-449e-57d5-b756-95e94c17a5b8" :step/math-academy-id 44590 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175712 :question/id #uuid "68b95ae7-c0ac-590c-a764-0500455ca70c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12412"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ec24aa-1f87-5ce1-8ae3-ae4cb5462ae6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363444} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532693}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Shifted Sphere or Part of a Shifted Sphere in Spherical Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "5e26990d-5383-595c-9264-f4683700a33e" :step/math-academy-id 44591}] :activity/title "Surfaces in Spherical Polar Coordinates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176120}]}
